---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'troubleshooting' as const;
---
<Docs lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Troubleshooting"
  description="Five common symptoms, what causes them, and what to do when the extension does not behave as expected.">
<div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>docs</b> / troubleshooting</p>
          <h1>When something looks wrong</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">Most of the behaviours that seem surprising at first are deliberate. For each case below, you'll find what you're seeing, why it happens, and what — if anything — you need to do.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>The toolbar doesn't appear</h2>
          <ul class="tick">
            <li><span class="mk n">·</span><b>The domain isn't authorized yet</b><span class="tx">The extension runs only where you have allowed it, github.com included: a fresh install is active nowhere until you grant a first domain from its options page. For a domain the extension doesn't already know — a self-hosted instance, an internal host — you also need to specify which platform it is running so the extension can select the correct adapter.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk n">·</span><b>The platform's page structure has changed</b><span class="tx">If the markup the extension relies on changes, it disables the affected area rather than risk interacting with the wrong element. The options page keeps a log of the last fifty occurrences, containing only the rule name and timestamp — never review content.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk n">·</span><b>The mode is <code>off</code></b><span class="tx">The organization or repository configuration can disable the extension entirely.</span></li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>“Configuration unread” banner</h2>
          <p>The extension could not retrieve <code>.conventional-comments.json</code>. It continues to assist, but because it cannot determine the repository's rules, it <strong>never blocks a comment from being submitted</strong>. That behaviour is intentional: a user should not be prevented from working because of a rule the extension itself could not read.</p>
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="ct">what this banner does not mean</span>
            <p style="margin:0">A repository with <strong>no <code>.conventional-comments.json</code> file at all</strong> is not an error or degraded state. In that case, the product defaults apply and no banner is shown. The banner only appears when there is configuration to retrieve but the extension cannot read it. For a public repository — or a private repository while you're authenticated — you should not normally see it. If you're signed out and the repository is private, the banner is accurately reporting that the configuration cannot be accessed.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2><code>/rebase</code> is being rejected</h2>
          <p>This is expected: <code>toolCommands</code> is empty by default, so nothing is exempted until the repository declares it.</p>
          <pre class="code"><span class="tk-k">"toolCommands"</span>: [<span class="tk-v">"/*"</span>, <span class="tk-v">"@dependabot"</span>, <span class="tk-v">"@renovate"</span>]</pre>
          <p><code>"/*"</code> recognizes every slash command as a tool command. Bot handles are declared individually and matched against the complete first token. For example, <code>@codex review</code> is recognized as a command, while <code>@codexplains why</code> is not.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>A bot's comments fail the check</h2>
          <p>Add the bot's platform login to <code>exemptUsers</code>. Matching is case-insensitive and uses the platform login, <strong>not</strong> the display name.</p>
          <p class="small">Platform-generated entries such as timeline events, branch updates, and system notifications are exempt automatically and do not need to be listed. An approval with <strong>no review text at all</strong> is also always exempt. Otherwise, simply approving a pull request without writing a comment would produce a validation failure — one the author of the pull request could not fix, because they cannot edit somebody else's review.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>A blocking point can't be resolved</h2>
          <p>A blocking thread can be resolved only by:</p>
          <ul class="bul">
            <li>the author of the blocking comment, or</li>
            <li>a member of the group configured for resolution overrides.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>If nobody can resolve it, the designated group is probably empty. That group is a prerequisite for <code>enforce</code> precisely to prevent a blocking thread from becoming impossible to resolve.</p>
          <p>The other option is a <code>decision</code> reply explaining why the point will not be addressed. A <code>decision</code> must contain at least twenty characters of justification. The explanation is recorded directly in the thread, so the override is always explicit and visible.</p>
        </section>
        <div class="pager"></div>
</Docs>
